+  <section class="clip" id="beat-01" data-beat-id="beat-01" data-track-index="0" data-start="0" data-duration="16.25"><div class="kicker">THE HARDEST WORK</div><h1 class="title">Your Transition Has <span class="accent">Two Sides</span></h1><p class="sub">Name what ends. Choose what travels.</p><p class="narration">In the last module, we named your transition. In this one, we do the hardest and most important work of the whole track — and it's work most career advice skips entirely.</p></section>
+  <section class="clip" id="beat-02" data-beat-id="beat-02" data-track-index="0" data-start="16.25" data-duration="16.65"><div class="kicker">EVERY REAL REINVENTION</div><div class="lanes"><div class="lane gold"><h3>01 · LETTING GO</h3><p>Release what no longer fits.</p></div><div class="lane"><h3>02 · CARRYING FORWARD</h3><p>Translate what still matters.</p></div></div><p class="narration">Every real career reinvention has two sides. There's a letting-go, and there's a carrying-forward. Most people try to do only one — and that's why so many transitions get stuck. Let's name both.</p></section>
+  <section class="clip" id="beat-03" data-beat-id="beat-03" data-track-index="0" data-start="32.9" data-duration="13.65"><div class="kicker">FIRST · LETTING GO</div><p class="quote">“Every transition begins with an <span class="accent">ending.</span> Not a beginning.”</p><p class="sub">William Bridges</p><p class="narration">First: letting go. William Bridges, whose work we referenced in Module 1, said something worth repeating. Every transition begins with an ending. Not a beginning. An ending.</p></section>
+  <section class="clip" id="beat-04" data-beat-id="beat-04" data-track-index="0" data-start="46.55" data-duration="23.65"><div class="kicker">WHEN THE ENDING GETS SKIPPED</div><div class="cards"><div class="card"><b>OLD TITLE</b><p>Old thinking follows you.</p></div><div class="card"><b>OLD JOB</b><p>Quiet grief comes along.</p></div><div class="card"><b>NEW START</b><p>Excitement cannot land.</p></div></div><p class="narration">Skip the ending — jump into the new role without acknowledging what you're leaving — and the old identity comes with you. You show up at the new company still thinking like your old title. You take the new job while quietly grieving the old one. You wonder why you don't feel excited, when the answer is that you haven't said goodbye yet.</p></section>
